@page "/performance"
@implements IDisposable

@* The numbers, and the one decision that matters more than any of them in a browser: compiling
   ahead of time. The live benchmark runs the interpreter-vs-AOT table's own operations in the
   reader's browser, so whether the site they are on was compiled ahead of time shows up in their
   own measurements rather than in a claim. *@

<PageTitle>Performance · Bmage</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Performance</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Every number here was measured with BenchmarkDotNet, not estimated - and in the browser, they only
        hold with <strong>ahead-of-time compilation</strong>. Read the first section before anything else.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Measure this browser</h3>
    <p>
        The operations from the table below, on a generated 1920 &times; 1080 image, best of three
        after a warm-up. Around five times the AOT column means the interpreter. This site is
        published with AOT; under <code>dotnet run</code> it is interpreted, and every row takes
        several times longer - which is the point.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" disabled="@(_busy || IsHydrating)" @onclick="RunAsync">
            <Icon Path="@Icons.Gauge" />
            Run the benchmark
        </button>
        @if (_busy && !_measuringEditing)
        {
            <span class="working" role="status"><span class="bb-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>Measuring @_current&hellip;</span>
        }
    </div>

    @if (_results.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="bb-scroll perf-results" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Benchmark results for this browser">
            <table class="bb-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Operation</th>
                        <th scope="col" class="demo-num">This browser</th>
                        <th scope="col" class="demo-num">Interpreter</th>
                        <th scope="col" class="demo-num">AOT</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var result in _results)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">@result.Name</th>
                            <td class="demo-num"><strong>@result.Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds.ToString("0") ms</strong></td>
                            <td class="demo-num">@result.Interpreter ms</td>
                            <td class="demo-num">@result.Aot ms</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    }
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Editing tools in this browser</h3>
    <p>
        The editor's straightening, colour, selection, painting and layer tools, on the same 1920 &times; 1080 image and
        a product shot of that size, best of three after a warm-up, beside what they cost in Chrome on the interpreter
        and compiled ahead of time, and on desktop .NET on one thread. Those were measured on a slower machine than the
        table below: the benchmark above took about twice as long there, so compare the rows of this table with each
