</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> WebAssembly: compile ahead of time</h3>
    <p>
        By default Blazor WebAssembly runs .NET code on an IL interpreter. Tight pixel loops are its
        worst case: every <code>Vector4</code> operation RyuJIT turns into one SIMD instruction becomes
        a separate interpreted step. Bmage's loops are written around that, and the browser does the
        decoding, encoding and the editor's working copy, but what stays in .NET is still several times
        faster compiled ahead of time. Measured on a 1920 &times; 1080 image in Chromium, best of three
        after a warm-up:
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Interpreter against AOT">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">Interpreter</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">AOT</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">Desktop .NET</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Reference)
                {
                    <tr><th scope="row">@row.Name</th><td class="demo-num">@row.Interpreter ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>@row.Aot ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">@row.Desktop ms</td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        The interpreter is 5 to 9&times; slower than desktop .NET; AOT is within 1 to 2.3&times; of it. A Debug
        build measured the same as Release on the interpreter, so the build configuration is not the
        issue. <a href="/hosting">Hosting models</a> shows how to switch it on, and how this site does.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AotSnippet" Language="XML" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Resizing</h3>
    <p>Downscaling to 1280px on the long edge, and generating a 200px thumbnail (desktop .NET 10, AVX2):</p>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Resize timings">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">1920&times;1080</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">3840&times;2160</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">6000&times;4000</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Box</th><td class="demo-num">9.8 ms</td><td class="demo-num">16.5 ms</td><td class="demo-num">63.1 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Bilinear</th><td class="demo-num">10.6 ms</td><td class="demo-num">20.6 ms</td><td class="demo-num">65.0 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Bicubic</th><td class="demo-num">13.4 ms</td><td class="demo-num">25.4 ms</td><td class="demo-num">76.1 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Lanczos3</th><td class="demo-num">16.0 ms</td><td class="demo-num">40.0 ms</td><td class="demo-num">82.2 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Thumbnail to 200px (Auto)</th><td class="demo-num">3.3 ms</td><td class="demo-num">8.1 ms</td><td class="demo-num">22.6 ms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Allocations stay between 23 KB and 185 KB regardless of source size, because pixel buffers are
        pooled and the resampler works a row at a time. Thumbnailing a 24 megapixel photo originally
        took 368 ms; an exact integer box prepass to within 2&times; of the target, finished by the
        requested filter, brought it to 52 ms and cut its allocations tenfold - with quality guarded by
        tests that a ramp stays linear and a fine checkerboard averages to grey rather than aliasing.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Operations and pipelines</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Operation timings">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">1920&times;1080</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">3840&times;2160</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Crop</th><td class="demo-num">0.06 ms</td><td class="demo-num">0.28 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Rotate 90 (lossless)</th><td class="demo-num">1.8 ms</td><td class="demo-num">27.2 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Brightness (one pass)</th><td class="demo-num">1.3 ms</td><td class="demo-num">5.1 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Four adjustments, fused</th><td class="demo-num">17.2 ms</td><td class="demo-num">67.4 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Four adjustments, unfused</th><td class="demo-num">18.4 ms</td><td class="demo-num">75.2 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Gaussian blur, radius 8</th><td class="demo-num">25.3 ms</td><td class="demo-num">99.5 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Unsharp mask</th><td class="demo-num">27.3 ms</td><td class="demo-num">107 ms</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Rotate 37&deg; (resampled)</th><td class="demo-num">65.6 ms</td><td class="demo-num">236 ms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>Fusing adjustments is worth about 1.1&times;.</strong> Adjustments that map each channel from itself, such as brightness, contrast and gamma, run through 256-entry tables whether fused or not, so fusing mostly saves passes over the pixels.</li>
        <li><strong>A right angle is 9 to 37&times; cheaper than an arbitrary one</strong>, because it moves pixels rather than resampling them. The optimiser routes multiples of 90&deg; to the lossless path automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Blur is independent of radius</strong>: three box passes approximating a gaussian, so radius 40 costs the same as radius 4. Below a radius of about 2, where whole-pixel boxes would miss by more than 10%, a sampled gaussian of at most 13 taps runs instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Resize then crop fuses</strong> into one resample of only the surviving region - 25.8 ms against 62.2 ms on a 4000 &times; 3000 source, bit-identical.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Codecs</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Codec timings">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">1024&times;768</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">3840&times;2160</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">Allocated (4K)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">PNG decode</th><td class="demo-num">8.7 ms</td><td class="demo-num">88 ms</td><td class="demo-num">37 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">JPEG decode</th><td class="demo-num">7.4 ms</td><td class="demo-num">77 ms</td><td class="demo-num">14 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">JPEG encode, quality 85</th><td class="demo-num">11.5 ms</td><td class="demo-num">122 ms</td><td class="demo-num">6.7 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">PNG encode, level 1</th><td class="demo-num">19.2 ms</td><td class="demo-num">198 ms</td><td class="demo-num">98 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">PNG encode, level 6</th><td class="demo-num">68.0 ms</td><td class="demo-num">720 ms</td><td class="demo-num">46 MB</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        PNG compression level matters more than anything else here: level 6 is 3.6&times; slower than
        level 1 for a modest size gain. Level 1 suits a preview that is on screen for a moment; the
        default of 6 suits the file the user keeps.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card" id="threads-workers-and-memory">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Threads, workers and memory</h3>
    <p>
        Every number above is on <strong>one thread</strong>, the default. Operations whose rows are
        independent - resampling, rotation and warps, per-pixel adjustments, convolution, median,
        vignette, masks, layer compositing and drawing images - split their rows over
        <code>MaxDegreeOfParallelism</code> threads, each band with scratch of its own, so the pixels are
        identical however many run; a test asserts it for every one of them. Measured on a 10 core,
        20 thread desktop:
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Parallel speed-ups">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">1 thread</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">20 threads</th><th scope="col" class="demo-num">Speed-up</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Downscale 6000&times;4000 to 1280, Lanczos3</th><td class="demo-num">251 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>54 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">4.7&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Rotate 3840&times;2160 by 37&deg;, bicubic</th><td class="demo-num">450 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>74 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">6.1&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Saturation and hue, 3840&times;2160</th><td class="demo-num">95 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>15 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">6.3&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Median, radius 1, 1920&times;1080</th><td class="demo-num">132 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>16 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">8.5&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Exposure through a gradient mask, 3840&times;2160</th><td class="demo-num">40 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>7.6 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">5.2&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Two masked layers composited, 3840&times;2160</th><td class="demo-num">487 ms</td><td class="demo-num"><strong>83 ms</strong></td><td class="demo-num">5.9&times;</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Blurs and sharpening stay on one thread: they stream rows through running sums, and splitting them
        would change the sums and so the pixels. One thread is the default because it is right for a
        server, where requests and circuits already run side by side, and for the browser, where
        WebAssembly runs .NET on the page's thread; a desktop app, a background job or a WebAssembly app
        built with <code>WasmEnableThreads</code> should raise it.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ThreadsSnippet" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <h4>Web Workers</h4>
    <p>
        In the browser, decoding, encoding and building the editor's working copy run in a small pool of
        Web Workers, so the page keeps painting and handling input while a photo decodes, and the several
        decodes a batch makes spread over several cores. The pool starts with one worker and grows, while
        all are busy, to one per spare core and at most four. Files and pixels are transferred, not
        copied. Without <code>OffscreenCanvas</code>, or where a Content Security Policy blocks workers,
        the same work runs on the page's thread. <code>MaxCodecWorkers</code> caps the pool, and 0 turns
        it off.
    </p>
    <h4>Memory you can predict</h4>
    <p>
        A pipeline estimates the most pixel memory its run will hold before it runs, following the
        buffers each step keeps - a test holds the estimate between what a run really allocates and twice
        that. A <code>BudgetedPixelAllocator</code> enforces a ceiling: every buffer and mask an operation
        makes comes from the allocator in its context, so one budget bounds a request, a session or a batch,
        on any number of threads, and an allocation that would pass it is refused with
        <code>ImageMemoryBudgetExceededException</code> before it is made. A batch can also admit
        images by their expected peak with <code>MaxMemoryBytes</code> - see <a href="/batch">Batch processing</a>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@MemorySnippet" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Interactive editing</h3>
    <p>
        The editor works on a proxy no larger than 2048px on its longest edge and replays the edit at
        full resolution on export: for a 24 megapixel photo, a 25&times; reduction in the pixels each
        preview touches - the difference between a slider that tracks your finger and one that does
        not. History stores descriptions, not pixels: a test asserts that 100 undo steps on a 6000
        &times; 4000 image stay under 1 MB, where pixel snapshots would need about 4.8 GB.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Guidance</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>Cap dimensions before anything else.</strong> Every later operation costs proportionally less.</li>
        <li><strong>Group adjustments together.</strong> Consecutive per-pixel operations fuse into one pass; a geometric operation between them prevents that.</li>
        <li><strong>Prefer right angles.</strong> <code>Rotate(90)</code> takes the lossless path; <code>Rotate(90.5)</code> resamples the whole image.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep batch concurrency low, or budget it.</strong> Each worker holds a decoded image; four on 24 megapixel photos is roughly 400 MB of live pixels. <code>MaxMemoryBytes</code> lets the size of each image decide instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Add threads where one image at a time should finish sooner</strong> - a desktop app or a background job - not on a busy server.</li>
        <li><strong>Dispose buffers</strong>, and <strong>use object URLs</strong>, not data URLs.</li>
    </ul>
    <CodeSnippet Code="ImagePipeline.Create().AutoOrient().MaxSize(1920, 1920).Sharpen(0.3f)" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

@code {
    private sealed record ReferenceRow(string Name, int Interpreter, int Aot, int Desktop);

    private sealed record Result(string Name, TimeSpan Elapsed, int Interpreter, int Aot);

    private static readonly ReferenceRow[] Reference =
    [
        new("Resize to 1280, Lanczos3", 108, 24, 15),
        new("Brightness", 11, 3, 1),
        new("Four adjustments, fused", 118, 23, 25),
        new("Gaussian blur, radius 8", 125, 24, 24),
        new("Unsharp mask", 203, 36, 25),
    ];

    // The same operations as the reference table, so the reader's numbers line up against it row by row.
    private static readonly (string Name, Func<ImagePipeline> Pipeline)[] Benchmarks =
    [
        ("Resize to 1280, Lanczos3", () => ImagePipeline.Create().Resize(1280, 1280, ResizeMode.Fit, ResamplingFilter.Lanczos3)),
        ("Brightness", () => ImagePipeline.Create().Brightness(0.2f)),
        ("Four adjustments, fused", () => ImagePipeline.Create().Brightness(0.1f).Contrast(0.15f).Saturation(0.2f).Vibrance(0.2f)),
        ("Gaussian blur, radius 8", () => ImagePipeline.Create().Blur(8f)),
        ("Unsharp mask", () => ImagePipeline.Create().Sharpen(0.5f)),
    ];

    private readonly List<Result> _results = [];

    private sealed record EditingReference(string Name, int? Interpreter, int? Aot, double Desktop);

    private sealed record EditingResult(string Name, TimeSpan Elapsed, EditingReference Reference);

    /// <summary>The images the editing tools are measured on, made once per run.</summary>
    private sealed class EditingImages : IDisposable
    {
        public EditingImages(int width, int height)
        {
            Photo = SampleImages.Landscape(width, height);
            // A product shot: a subject on a near-white backdrop, for the background removal and trimming.
            Product = ImageBuffer.Create(width, height, new Rgba32(248, 248, 246));
            Product.Fill(new System.Drawing.Rectangle(width / 4, height / 5, width / 2, height * 3 / 5), new Rgba32(40, 90, 160));
            var rng = new Random(7);
            Paint = new PaintLayer([.. Enumerable.Range(0, 20).Select(i => new PaintStroke(
                [.. Enumerable.Range(0, 40).Select(j => new Vector2(rng.Next(width), rng.Next(height)) * 0.1f + new Vector2(j * width / 50f, i * height / 25f))],
                24, new Rgba32(200, 40, 40)) { Hardness = 0.6f })]);
        }

        public ImageBuffer Photo { get; }

        public ImageBuffer Product { get; }

        public PaintLayer Paint { get; }

        public void Dispose()
        {
            Photo.Dispose();
            Product.Dispose();
        }
    }

    // The operations of Tests/Bit.Bmage.Benchmarks' EditingToolBenchmarks, so a row lines up with its desktop number.
    // The interpreter and AOT columns were measured with the opt-in PerformanceMeasurements browser test, best of two
    // runs of this page (docs/performance.md, WebAssembly: editing tools).
    private static readonly (EditingReference Reference, Func<EditingImages, IDisposable?> Run)[] EditingBenchmarks =
    [
        (new("Canvas: 40px border", 3, 1, 1.6), i => Apply(CanvasSizeOperation.Margins(40, Rgba32.White), i.Photo)),
        (new("Whites and blacks", 21, 7, 3.6), i => Apply(new WhitesBlacksAdjustment(0.3f, -0.2f), i.Photo)),
        (new("Trim bounds", 44, 1, 1.3), i => { ContentBounds.Find(i.Product); return null; }),
        (new("Auto white balance", 43, 15, 15.4), i => Apply(new AutoWhiteBalanceOperation(), i.Photo)),
        (new("Expand a selection by 10px", 349, 91, 27.9), i => new EllipseMask(new System.Drawing.RectangleF(100, 100, 800, 600)).Expand(10).Render(i.Photo.Size)),
        (new("Duotone", 152, 28, 28.4), i => Apply(GradientMapOperation.Duotone(new Rgba32(20, 30, 90), new Rgba32(250, 210, 120)), i.Photo)),
        (new("Vibrance, for reference", 161, 26, 35.1), i => Apply(new VibranceAdjustment(0.4f), i.Photo)),
        (new("Colorize", 105, 46, 50.6), i => Apply(new ColorizeAdjustment(30, 0.4f), i.Photo)),
        (new("High pass, radius 3", 366, 66, 64.3), i => Apply(new HighPassFilter(3), i.Photo)),
        (new("Remove a plain background", 714, 102, 66.8), i => Apply(new AlphaMaskOperation(new FloodMask([new(0, 0), new(1, 0), new(0, 1), new(1, 1)]) { Units = MaskUnits.Relative, Inverted = true }), i.Product)),
        (new("HSL, two bands", 758, 87, 93.7), i => Apply(new HslAdjustment().With(HueBand.Blues, new HslShift(-10, 0.3f, -0.2f)).With(HueBand.Greens, new HslShift(15, -0.2f, 0)), i.Photo)),
        (new("Straighten 5°", 1684, 88, 122), i => Apply(new StraightenOperation(5), i.Photo)),
        (new("Paint layer, 20 long strokes", 1383, 179, 134), i => Composite(i.Photo, i.Paint)),
        (new("Motion blur 20px", 618, 267, 142), i => Apply(new MotionBlurFilter(20, 0), i.Photo)),
        (new("Isolated group: paint and a multiplied fill", 3281, 372, 225), i => Composite(i.Photo,
            new GroupLayer([i.Paint, new FillLayer(new Rgba32(250, 220, 160)) { BlendMode = BlendMode.Multiply }]) { Isolated = true })),
        (new("A fill clipped to the paint layer", 2313, 327, 200), i => Composite(i.Photo, i.Paint, new FillLayer(new Rgba32(40, 90, 200)) { ClipToLayerBelow = true })),
    ];

    private static ImageBuffer Apply(IImageOperation operation, ImageBuffer source) => operation.Apply(source, OperationContext.Default.WithMutation(false));

    private static ImageBuffer Composite(ImageBuffer source, params Layer[] layers)
    {
        var target = source.Clone();
        LayerCompositor.Composite(target, layers);
        return target;
    }

    private readonly List<EditingResult> _editing = [];
    private bool _measuringEditing;
    private string _current = "";
    private bool _busy;
    private bool _disposed;

    private bool IsHydrating => RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false;

    private const string AotSnippet = """
        <PropertyGroup>
          <RunAOTCompilation>true</RunAOTCompilation>
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;

    private const string ThreadsSnippet = """
        // For every operation the services run: processors, loaders and editors.
        builder.Services.AddBitBmageServices(options =>
        {
            options.MaxDegreeOfParallelism = Environment.ProcessorCount;
            options.MaxCodecWorkers = 2;          // the browser codecs' Web Workers; 0 keeps them on the page
        });

        // For one run:
        using var result = pipeline.Execute(image, new PipelineExecutionOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 8 });

        // In a batch every image in flight uses that many: MaxConcurrency × MaxDegreeOfParallelism threads.
        """;

    private const string MemorySnippet = """
        var estimate = pipeline.EstimateMemory(new Size(6000, 4000));
        estimate.PeakBytes;     // the most pixel memory the run holds at once, its result included
        estimate.OutputBytes;   // the result

        // A hard ceiling: an allocation past it throws ImageMemoryBudgetExceededException before it is made.
        var budget = new BudgetedPixelAllocator(256L * 1024 * 1024);
        using var result = pipeline.Execute(source, new PipelineExecutionOptions { Allocator = budget });
        Console.WriteLine($"{budget.PeakBytes / 1048576.0:0.0} MB at the peak");
        """;

    private async Task RunAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        _results.Clear();
        _current = "the source";
        StateHasChanged();
        await Task.Yield();

        using var source = SampleImages.Landscape(1920, 1080);

        for (var i = 0; i < Benchmarks.Length && !_disposed; i++)
        {
            var (name, pipeline) = Benchmarks[i];
            _current = name.ToLowerInvariant();
            StateHasChanged();
            await Task.Yield();

            // One warm-up, then the best of three: the first run pays for JIT-free but cold code paths
            // and pool growth, which is not what the row is measuring.
            var best = TimeSpan.MaxValue;
            for (var attempt = 0; attempt < 4 && !_disposed; attempt++)
            {
                var started = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
                using (pipeline().Execute(source)) { }
                started.Stop();
                if (attempt > 0 && started.Elapsed < best) best = started.Elapsed;

                // Let the page breathe between runs on a single-threaded runtime.
                await Task.Yield();
            }

            var reference = Reference[i];
            _results.Add(new Result(name, best, reference.Interpreter, reference.Aot));
        }

        _busy = false;
    }

    private async Task RunEditingAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        _measuringEditing = true;
        _editing.Clear();
        _current = "the images";
        StateHasChanged();
        await Task.Yield();

        using var images = new EditingImages(1920, 1080);
        foreach (var (reference, run) in EditingBenchmarks)
        {
            if (_disposed) break;
            _current = reference.Name.ToLowerInvariant();
            StateHasChanged();
            await Task.Yield();

            // One warm-up, then the best of three, as above.
            var best = TimeSpan.MaxValue;
            for (var attempt = 0; attempt < 4 && !_disposed; attempt++)
            {
                var started = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
                run(images)?.Dispose();
                started.Stop();
                if (attempt > 0 && started.Elapsed < best) best = started.Elapsed;
                await Task.Yield();
            }
            _editing.Add(new EditingResult(reference.Name, best, reference));
        }

        _busy = false;
        _measuringEditing = false;
    }

    public void Dispose() => _disposed = true;
}
